WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.059 line:-0.9 align:center
We introduce Proxy Policy Steering,

2
00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:06.161 line:-0.9 align:center
an inference-time adaptation method for robotics foundation models.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.073 line:-0.9 align:center
Generalist policies carry broad manipulation priors from large-scale data.

4
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.380 line:-0.9 align:center
However, they still struggle in downstream tasks that

5
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.320 line:-0.9 align:center
are underrepresented in the training distribution.

6
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.800 line:-0.9 align:center
Direct fine-tuning methods tackle this by modifying the base policy

7
00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.480 line:-0.9 align:center
weights, reshaping the base policy’s action distribution.

8
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.461 line:-0.9 align:center
While this effectively adapts the policy behavior,

9
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.014 line:-0.9 align:center
it can overfit to the limited demonstrations and lose the general priors.

10
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.340 line:-0.9 align:center
Existing steering methods, like DSRL, change the initial noise or

11
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.680 line:-0.9 align:center
flow-matching process to select actions suitable for the task.

12
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.200 line:-0.9 align:center
However, the actions are still sampled from the base

13
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.064 line:-0.9 align:center
distribution and do not deliver novel behaviors.

14
00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.469 line:-0.9 align:center
How could generalist policies learn new skills without losing the base prior?

15
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.920 line:-0.9 align:center
We propose Proxy Policy Steering.

16
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.380 line:-0.9 align:center
Instead of reshaping the base action distribution,

17
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.300 line:-0.9 align:center
we estimate how task supervision would change it.

18
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.980 line:-0.9 align:center
Specifically, we distill the base policy into a lightweight

19
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.373 line:-0.9 align:center
reference proxy under task observations.

20
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.912 line:-0.9 align:center
We then fine-tune this proxy under task supervision to obtain a task proxy.

21
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.620 line:-0.9 align:center
At inference, we take the velocity predictions of all three models,

22
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.072 line:-0.9 align:center
subtract the reference velocity and add the task velocity.

23
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.240 line:-0.9 align:center
This residual captures the task-induced shift,

24
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.398 line:-0.9 align:center
steering the policy to approximate the fine-tuned action distribution.

25
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.400 line:-2 align:center
Facing a state where the base policy needs guidance to locate the correct

26
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.600 line:-2 align:center
grasping position, the residual steers the base toward the task proxy,

27
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:38.780 line:-2 align:center
which guides the robot to show novel task behavior for correct grasping.

28
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.800 line:-2 align:center
In a state that is not covered in the task demonstrations due to

29
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.605 line:-2 align:center
a failed grasp, the base policy shows generic recovery behavior.

30
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.274 line:-2 align:center
Here, our proxy policies share the same overfitting errors, making the residual near zero.

31
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:56.772 line:-2 align:center
Therefore, the base retains control and recovers from the grasping failure.

32
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:06.774 line:-2 align:center
When the proxy policies agree with the base policy. The robot follows the shared behavior.

33
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.240 line:-0.9 align:center
By combining proxy residual with the base prior, PPS injects task guidance while retaining

34
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.456 line:-0.9 align:center
the general priors of the base model, achieving robust manipulation in downstream tasks.

35
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.020 line:-0.9 align:center
We evaluate PPS on 12 tasks, spanning long-horizon goals,

36
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.495 line:-0.9 align:center
deformable and articulated objects, and diverse skills.

37
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:32.128 line:-0.9 align:center
PPS outperforms LoRA fine-tuning, previous steering methods, and trained-from-scratch policies.

38
00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.140 line:-0.9 align:center
Since PPS keeps the base model intact, it preserves the general priors from the base model.

39
00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.180 line:-0.9 align:center
This helps the policy recover under human interventions even though

40
00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.514 line:-0.9 align:center
no recovery behavior is collected in the demonstrations.

41
00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.360 line:-0.9 align:center
Whereas LoRA fine-tuning can overfit to the small-scale task-specific

42
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.193 line:-0.9 align:center
demonstrations and overwrite parts of the manipulation priors learned

43
00:02:55.233 --> 00:02:58.763 line:-0.9 align:center
during pre-training, causing it to fail under perturbations.

44
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.300 line:-0.9 align:center
PPS also enables injecting information from additional modalities into the base model,

45
00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.540 line:-0.9 align:center
such as using point cloud to locate textureless objects and

46
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.462 line:-0.9 align:center
leveraging audio inputs to find the sound source.

47
00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.436 line:-0.9 align:center
Thanks for watching!
